{
  "rule_id": "R43_155",
  "target": "ts",
  "tier": "T2",
  "governed_construct": "A catch clause (CatchClause node) of a try statement, i.e. try { ... } catch ... { ... }. Each catch clause in the file is one instance. Promise .catch(...) method calls and try/finally without a catch clause are not instances.",
  "interpretation": "The rule asks that error handling via try-catch use a typed catch clause: the catch clause must declare a binding and that binding must carry an explicit type annotation (in TypeScript the only legal annotations are `unknown` and `any`; `unknown` is the idiomatic form, and any explicit annotation counts as conforming). A catch clause whose binding has no type annotation (`catch (err)`), an optional catch binding with no parameter at all (`catch { ... }`), and an unannotated destructuring binding (`catch ({ message })`) all violate. A file is SATISFIED when it has at least one catch clause and every catch clause is typed, VIOLATED when any catch clause is untyped (including nested ones), and NOT_APPLICABLE when it contains no catch clause. Mentions of catch in comments or strings, and Promise.prototype.catch calls, are not instances.",
